# Contributing to deinterleaving-by-smart-scan

Thanks for your interest in contributing to this SIH 2026 (Problem Statement
#26055) project! This document covers how to report issues, propose changes,
and submit pull requests.

## Getting Started

1. Fork the repository and clone your fork:
   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/deinterleaving-by-smart-scan.git
   cd deinterleaving-by-smart-scan
   ```
2. Create a virtual environment and install dependencies:
   ```bash
   python3 -m venv .venv
   source .venv/bin/activate      # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
   pip install -r requirements.txt
   ```
3. Create a branch for your change:
   ```bash
   git checkout -b feature/short-description
   ```

## Project Layout

The core pipeline modules (`sim.py`, `scheduler.py`, `deinterleave.py`,
`identify.py`, `evaluate.py`, `data_io.py`, `mini_h5.py`, `run_all.py`) import
each other directly (e.g. `scheduler.py` does `from sim import Twin,
Emitter`), so please keep them as siblings in the same folder rather than
moving individual files into subfolders — that will break imports.

- `out/` — generated schedules, allocations, and prototype data. Don't hand-edit
  files here; they're produced by running the pipeline.
- `proto/` — the interactive HTML demo (`build.py` + `template.html`). It
  reads `../out/prototype_data.json`, so `proto/` and `out/` must stay
  siblings of each other.

## Reporting Issues

- Search [existing issues](../../issues) before opening a new one.
- Include steps to reproduce, expected behavior, and actual behavior.
- For scheduling/deinterleaving bugs, describe (or attach) the scenario file
  used, e.g. which `config_*.h5`.

## Making Changes

- Keep pull requests focused on a single change.
- Add or update docstrings/comments for any new functions.
- If you change scheduling or deinterleaving logic, re-run
  ```bash
  python run_all.py
  ```
  and update `docs/results.md` (or `requirements.md`) if the metrics change.
- Follow the existing code style (PEP 8 for Python).

## Submitting a Pull Request

1. Push your branch and open a PR against `main`.
2. Describe **what** changed and **why** in the PR description.
3. Link any related issues.
4. Be ready to respond to review comments — small follow-up commits are fine.

## Code of Conduct

By participating in this project, you agree to abide by our
[Code of Conduct](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md).
